course: everyday
lesson: 319
title: Anger, fears (films, needles, thunder, spiders, deep water, the news, getting lost) and boredom
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Feeling angry or scared is normal. What matters is what you do with the feeling: anger can be said calmly in words, and fear grows smaller when you talk about it, learn the facts and have a plan.
explain: Many fears shrink when you know the truth: a scary film is made up with special effects, a vaccine needle stings for about a second, and counting between a lightning flash and the thunder shows if a storm is moving away.
explain: Courage grows by small steps. Nobody jumps into the deep end on day one: you learn with a teacher, a little at a time, with an adult watching.
explain: Even boredom is useful. A rainy afternoon with no screen can become a den, a story, a drawing or a cake.
fact: When you are in a rage, close the door gently and use your words once you are calm; slamming can trap fingers, break things and frighten others.
fact: If something is truly unfair, say calmly what is unfair and what you would like to happen instead, because people listen to a calm, clear reason more than to a shout.
fact: After a frightening film, talk about it with a parent and do something calm and cheerful before bed; films are made up with special effects.
fact: If you are scared of a vaccination needle, tell the nurse, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand; the sting lasts a second and the vaccine protects you from serious illnesses.
fact: In a thunderstorm, stay indoors away from windows and keep busy with something cosy; inside a house you are safe.
fact: Counting the seconds between the flash and the bang shows how far away the storm is; if the count gets longer, it is moving away.
fact: If you are afraid of spiders or insects, keep your distance, ask an adult to move it outside and learn which local ones are harmless.
fact: Most spiders and insects are harmless, but in Australia some spiders are dangerous, so leave them to an adult.
fact: Learn to swim in the deep end with a teacher, step by step, and only swim where an adult is watching.
fact: If the news about wars, fires or disasters scares you, ask an adult to explain and watch less news; the news shows the worst things and often not the helpers.
fact: Agree a meeting place with your family in a crowd; if you get lost, ask a staff member or a parent with children for help.
fact: When you are bored with no screen, build a den, read, draw, write a story, do a puzzle or help cook; boredom is the start of creativity.
exercise: A boy is furious with his sister and runs to his room. What should he do with the door? | Close it gently and talk once he is calm | Slam it as hard as he can so the anger comes out | Kick it shut with his foot | Slam it twice so she knows
exercise: Why is slamming a door a bad idea? | It can trap fingers, break things and frighten others | Doors are made to be slammed, so it does them and nobody else any harm | It is only bad if the door is glass | It makes the anger last longer, nothing else
exercise: A girl's team is blamed for a mess another group made. What is the best way to deal with it? | Say calmly what is unfair and what she would like to happen | Shout at the teacher until she gives in and takes the blame off them | Mess up the other group's table | Say nothing and sulk all day
exercise: Why does a calm explanation work better than shouting? | People listen to a calm, clear reason much more | Shouting is always against school rules | Teachers cannot hear quiet voices | Calm people always win every argument
exercise: After a scary film at a sleepover, a boy cannot stop thinking about the monster. What will help? | Talking to a parent and doing something cheerful before bed | Watching the film again alone in the dark, so he gets used to the monster | Keeping the fear a secret | Staying awake all night to keep watch
exercise: What is TRUE about monsters in films? | They are made up with costumes and special effects | Some of them are real animals, but very rare and hidden away | They only exist in other countries | They come out when films are over
exercise: A girl is very scared of her vaccination at the doctor's. What can she do? | Tell the nurse, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand | Run out of the room before her turn | Hold her breath and stare at the needle the whole time so she is ready for it | Refuse to say anything and cry quietly
exercise: Why is a vaccination worth the sting? | The sting lasts a second and protects you from serious illnesses | It makes you grow taller and stronger over the next few years of school | It stops you ever getting a cold | It is only for grown-ups to feel good
exercise: A loud thunderstorm starts at night in Florida and a boy is scared. Where is safe? | Indoors, away from the windows | Outside under a tall tree | Standing at the window to watch | In the garden shed with the door open
exercise: You see lightning and count to 8 before the thunder. Next time you count to 15. What does this show? | The storm is moving away | The storm is coming closer | The storm is getting stronger | Lightning and thunder are not connected
exercise: There is a big spider on the bathroom wall in Ohio. What is a good thing to do? | Keep your distance and ask an adult to move it outside | Squash it flat with your bare hand before it runs away | Pick it up to look at it closely | Scream and run out of the house
exercise: Why can learning about local spiders and insects help with fear? | Most are harmless, and knowing the facts shrinks the fear | Learning about them makes them leave your house | All spiders are deadly everywhere, so fear is always right | Insects only bite people who are afraid
exercise: A girl wants to swim in the deep end but feels scared. What is the best plan? | Learn step by step with a teacher, with an adult watching | Jump in alone at the deep end to get it over with quickly | Wait until nobody is around to try | Never go in the pool again
exercise: A boy sees news about a wildfire and cannot sleep. What will help? | Ask an adult to explain and watch less news | Watch all the news channels to know more | Keep it to himself so nobody worries | Pack a bag and keep it by his bed every night
exercise: Why can the news seem scarier than the world really is? | It shows the worst things and often not the helpers | It is all made up like a film, with actors playing the people | It only shows things from long ago | It is meant for adults who are never scared
exercise: Before going into a busy fair, what should a family do? | Agree a meeting place in case anyone gets lost | Buy a big bag of snacks to share before going in | Leave phones at home | Split up to see more at once
exercise: A boy is lost at a big shopping centre. Who is a good person to ask for help? | A staff member or a parent with children | The first teenager he sees outside | Nobody, he should look alone | Someone in the car park
exercise: It is a rainy Saturday and the TV is broken. A girl says she is bored. What is a good idea? | Build a den, read, draw or help cook | Sit and wait until the TV is fixed | Complain until someone lends a phone | Go to sleep until the rain stops
test: A girl is angry because her brother broke her model. Which is the best thing to say? | «I'm upset you broke it. Please help me fix it.» | «I hate you!», and slam the door | Nothing at all, but quietly break one of his toys later | «You always ruin everything!», shouting
test: After a horror film at a friend's house, a boy is afraid of the dark. Which is TRUE? | Talking about it makes the fear smaller | Talking about it makes it come true | Being scared means the film was real | He should never tell anyone about it
test: A boy says a flu vaccination hurts for a whole day. What is closer to the truth? | The sting lasts about a second, and it protects you | It hurts for a whole week, but it is still worth it | It never hurts at all | It hurts as long as the illness would
test: During a storm, a girl in Colorado counts 3 seconds, then 5, then 9 between flash and bang. What is happening? | The storm is moving away from her | The storm is right overhead and staying | The storm is coming towards her | She is counting too slowly
test: A boy in Western Australia finds a big black spider in the shed. What should he do? | Leave it alone and tell an adult | Catch it in his hands to show his friends | Poke it with a stick to see if it moves | Ignore it, all spiders are harmless
test: At a crowded beach in California, a girl cannot see her parents. If her family had a plan, what would she do? | Go to the meeting place they agreed | Wander up and down the beach shouting | Get into the sea to look from there | Walk home alone
test: A friend has been watching the news about floods every night and is scared. What is good advice? | Ask an adult to explain and watch less of it | Watch even more so you are ready | Keep the TV on in your room all night | Do not mention it to anyone
test: Which is TRUE about courage? | It grows by small steps, like learning to swim bit by bit | You either have it from birth or you never will, however hard you try | It means never feeling scared | It means doing dangerous things alone
test: What is TRUE about boredom? | It can be the start of being creative | It is always a waste of time | It only goes away with a screen | It is a sign that something is wrong
explain: A boy in Ireland swam in a deep lake to show he was not scared; the cold water tired him fast, and a man in a boat had to pull him out.
explain: A girl in Texas got lost at a fair and hid because she was frightened; it took much longer to find her than if she had asked a staff member.
fact: Fear of deep water is helpful: deep and cold water is truly dangerous, even for good swimmers.
fact: A lost child who asks a staff member or a parent with children is usually found quickly.
test: scared of deep water but they're calling me a baby | Stay out; your fear is protecting you | Jump in to prove them wrong | Go in without telling an adult | Swim out further than them
test: lost at the fair and kinda scared | Ask a staff member or a mum with kids | Hide until it's quiet | Leave the fair | Follow someone who says he knows your mum
test: spider in my room!!! gonna squish it with my hand | Leave it and ask an adult to move it | Squish it with your hand | Pick it up by its leg | Spray it with deodorant
test: did u hear a boy was bored and went "exploring" at the quarry pool and "nearly drowned in the freezing water". lesson? | Deep, cold water is truly dangerous; find something safe to do | Bored kids should explore water | Quarry pools are fine in summer, once the water has warmed up | Good swimmers can't drown
test: my mate says if you count after the lightning "and get to 5 it's gonna hit your house next". what's wrong | The count shows how far away the storm is, not where it will strike | Counting predicts exactly which house the lightning will strike next | Five seconds means it's on your roof | Counting makes lightning come closer
test: kids say the girl who's scared of spiders "is a total baby". pass it on? | No, lots of people fear spiders; don't spread it | Yes, she is a baby | Yes, and put a spider in her bag | Yes, and laugh at her
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of an increasing interval between lightning and thunder? | The storm is moving away | The storm is approaching | The storm has ended entirely | The lightning is becoming stronger
exam: Identify the principal reason for which news may appear more frightening than the world truly is. | It shows the worst things and often not the helpers | It is always untrue, being written to sell more newspapers | It is made with special effects | It is broadcast only at night
exam: It is given that a child is lost in a crowd. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | To ask a staff member or a parent with children for help | To leave the venue at once and walk home by the quickest way | To hide until the crowd has gone | To follow any adult who calls them
exam: State the activity appropriate for a child who is bored and has no screen. | Building a den, reading, drawing or helping to cook | Searching for excitement in risky places | Climbing onto the roof | Exploring a building site
